Motivated by 2D CenterNet [14], CenterPoint [57] is applied to 3D detection and tracking. It converts the sparse output of a backbone network into a map-view dense feature map and predicts a dense heatmap of the center locations of objects, based on the dense feature. This dense centerbased prediction has been adopted by several dense-head approaches [30, 33]. In this paper, we take a new direction and surprisingly show that a simple and strong sparse CNN is sufficient for direct prediction. The notable finding is that the dense head is not always necessary.
